<sn><md>#### Grazing Food Chain

* The food chain starts from living plants, goes through herbivores, and ends in carnivores.
* Ecosystems with such type of food chain are directly dependent on an influx of solar radiation.
* This type of chain thus depends on autotrophic energy capture and the movement of this captured energy to herbivores.
* Most of the ecosystems in nature follow this type of food chain.

**Example:**

* $\text{Grass} \rightarrow \text{Grass hopper} \rightarrow \text{Lizard} \rightarrow \text{Hawk}$ *(Grassland)*
* $\text{Phytoplankton} \rightarrow \text{Zooplankton} \rightarrow \text{Fish} \rightarrow \text{Snake}$ *(Pond)*

![diagram](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Ecology%20and%20Ecotourism/02.Ecosystem/assets/grazing-food-chain-diagram.png)</md></sn><sn><md>#### Detritus Food Chain

* This type of food chain goes from dead organic matter into microorganisms and then to organisms feeding on detritus (detrivores) and their predators.
* Such ecosystems are thus less dependent on direct solar energy.
* This depends chiefly on the influx of organic matter produced in another system.

For example, such type of food chain operates in the decomposing accumulated litter in a temperate forest.

$$\text{Dead leaves} \xrightarrow{\text{eaten by}} \text{Woodlouse} \xrightarrow{\text{eaten by}} \text{Blackbird}$$

$$\text{Detritus} \longrightarrow \text{Primary Detritus Feeder} \longrightarrow \text{Secondary Detritus Feeder}$$

![diagram](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Ecology%20and%20Ecotourism/02.Ecosystem/assets/detrious_food_chain.png)
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Significance of Food Chain

1. The studies of food chain help to understand the feeding relationship and the interaction between organisms in any ecosystem.
2. They also help us to appreciate the energy flow mechanism and matter circulation in ecosystem and understand the movement of toxic substances in the ecosystem.
3. The study of food chain helps us to understand the problems of bio-magnifications.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Food Web Overview

Definition and Concept

&gt; A **food web** consists of multiple interconnected food chains within an ecosystem or biome. Key characteristics include:

* **Complex Connections:** Unlike a single food chain, a food web illustrates that organisms derive energy from multiple sources.
* **Energy Flow:** It tracks how energy moves through different species within a community.
* **Consumer-Resource System:** Every organism participates in several overlapping food chains, forming an integrated consumer-resource network.

Historical Origin

* **Pioneer:** Introduced by ecologist **Charles Elton (1927)**, who originally referred to the concept as a **"food cycle."**
* **Eltonian Framework:**
* Herbivores derive energy directly or indirectly from plants (which capture sunlight).
* Carnivores prey on herbivores and may subsequently be preyed upon by higher-level carnivores.
* The cycle reaches a terminus at apex predators that have no natural enemies.
* All interconnected food chains in a community collectively form the complete system.

![diagram](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Ecology%20and%20Ecotourism/02.Ecosystem/assets/food%20web%20diagram.png)</md></sn>